<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hasan-i Sabbah</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1124">1124</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hasan-i Sabbah was an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismailism">Ismaili</a> missionary who founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizari-ismailism">Nizari</a> branch after the succession crisis of 1094 in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate</a>. From the fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alamut">Alamut</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alborz">Alborz</a> Mountains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> he built a network of strongholds. The Nizaris, known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/order-of-assassins">Assassins</a>, are part of the lasting Ismaili legacy of the Fatimids.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hasan-i-sabbah.jpg" alt="Hasan-i Sabbah" width="265" height="332" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Asabah2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Very little is known of Hasan's early life. The main source is Sargozasht-i Seyyednā, possibly his autobiography, according to which he was born in Qom in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> to a Twelver Shia family. The family soon moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rayy">Rayy</a>, where he studied languages, philosophy, astronomy and mathematics, especially geometry. Rayy was also a home of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismailism">Ismaili</a> missionaries, whose mission was directed from the Fatimid caliphate in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>.</p><p>At the age of 17 he converted to Ismailism and swore allegiance to the Fatimid caliph. The chief missionary of the region, 'Abdu l-Malik ibn Attash, made him a deputy missionary and advised him to study in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>. Hasan left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rayy">Rayy</a> in 1076 and reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> on 30 August 1078 after a roundabout journey. He stayed about three years and supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizari-ismailism">Nizar</a>, the caliph's elder son, as the next <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a>. After he displeased the army chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badr-al-jamali">Badr al-Jamali</a>, he was briefly imprisoned and deported, and he reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a> in 1081.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Hasan then toured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> on behalf of the mission. When the vizier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizam-al-mulk">Nizam al-Mulk</a> sent soldiers to capture him, he withdrew into the Alborz Mountains. In 1088 he found the castle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alamut">Alamut</a> in the Rudbar area, which guarded a valley about fifty kilometres long and five wide. His followers took it, according to the account without significant bloodshed, by first winning over the villages of the valley and then infiltrating the fort, in 1090.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alamut">Alamut</a> became the headquarters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizari-ismailism">Nizari</a> mission. Hasan lived there austerely, studying, writing and directing the mission, and he declared Persian the language of Nizari holy literature, so that Nizari writing from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> was set down in Persian for centuries. Legend says he never left his quarters except twice to climb to the roof, but this is considered highly doubtful, given his work in organising the movement.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Hasan died on 12 June 1124. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizari-ismailism">Nizari</a> followers called him Sayyidna, Our Master, and visited his mausoleum in Rudbar until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> demolished it. Although the movement came to be associated with assassination, Hasan did not introduce it as a religious duty, since other sects had used it, and it is not clear that he advocated it himself. A Nizari assassin is called a fida'i, a devotee. Western tradition, through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marco-polo">Marco Polo</a>, called the leaders of the movement the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashid-al-din-sinan">Old Man of the Mountain</a>. The Mongols captured and destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alamut">Alamut</a> in the 13th century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hasan-i Sabbah” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Nizari branch that split away following the 1094 succession crisis was led in Iran by <b>Hasan-i Sabbah</b> from the fortress of Alamut in the Alborz mountains.</blockquote><a href="/empire/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Nizari Ismailis operated from mountain fortresses such as Alamut, seized by <b>Hasan-i Sabbah</b> in 1090.</blockquote><a href="/empire/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Hasan-i Sabbah</b>, an Ismaili missionary, seized the castle of Alamut in the Alborz in 1090.</blockquote><a href="/state/nizari-ismaili-state">Nizari Ismaili state</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Qom, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. Died in Alamut Castle, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.64,50.88&amp;year=1114" aria-label="Qom on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Hasan-i Sabbah"><image href="/maps/globes/g060n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4361 4578h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4361 4578h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.64,50.88&amp;year=1114">What was here in 1114? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
